WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.333
A full spoken exchange with no server behind it.

2
00:00:02.333 --> 00:00:07.000
This episode covers how the browser build hears you and how it talks back, entirely client-side.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:13.294
Click the mic and mic dot ts captures your voice with getUserMedia, no plugin, no native permission dialog — just the browser's own microphone prompt.

4
00:00:13.294 --> 00:00:19.000
By default the browser build hands that audio straight to the browser's own built-in speech recognition — fast, and nothing to download.

5
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:29.588
If that's not available — an older browser, a locked-down one — it falls back to the same record-then-transcribe path as desktop: a 16 kilohertz WAV written into OPFS, transcribed client-side with transformers dot js Whisper.

6
00:00:29.588 --> 00:00:31.000
Same behavior, one extra step.

7
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:43.000
For the reply, speak dot ts gives you three options: Kokoro, which is already WASM so it just runs; any cloud voice engine you've configured; or the browser's own SpeechSynthesis as the built-in, always-available fallback.

8
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:53.000
Put it together and it's a full spoken exchange — ask a question out loud, hear the answer back — in a plain tab, with no Tauri process, no native app, nothing installed.

9
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Worth remembering: browser SpeechRecognition quality and language coverage varies by browser and OS.

10
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.000
If it feels off, Whisper is still there as the consistent, model-based fallback.

11
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.588
Voice works with no server.

12
00:01:03.588 --> 00:01:08.000
Next, a much bigger claim: real Python, running inside the browser tab too.

